  date: "2026-10-08",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 058: Sparse models can reduce calculation, but keeping many possible experts ready can still require substantial memory and communication.

Lesson 059: A low token price matters only when the service reliably produces answers that meet the customer’s quality and speed requirements.

Lesson 060: The cheapest useful AI service minimizes total hardware and operating effort, not merely the price of one accelerator.

Lesson 061: Chip requirements should begin with the customer result and the physical bottleneck, then determine what the silicon must contain.

Lesson 062: Design tools, reusable IP, verification and foundry rules must work together before a chip idea becomes manufacturable.

Tapeout is a starting gun, not a finish line

Imagine our accelerator team completes verification on Friday and releases the final design files to the foundry. Sales has customers waiting. Can finished accelerators ship the following week?

No. Tapeout means the design is ready to enter manufacturing. It does not mean a product exists. The design still has to become masks, patterned wafers, working dies, assembled packages and qualified systems. Each stage adds physical work, waiting time and another chance to discover a problem.

This is a simplified flow. Exact steps and timing vary by process, package, product and company.

The design must become manufacturing instructions

At tapeout, the final layout is used to create the photomasks that guide lithography. A mask is a precisely patterned plate that transfers one layer of the chip design onto a wafer. Modern chips require many patterned layers, so the manufacturing system needs a coordinated mask set rather than one picture of the whole chip.

The foundry also prepares the production route: which process steps, tools and checks each wafer lot will follow. A late design correction can require changed mask data and new checks. That is why the signoff discipline from Lesson 062 matters even after the architecture is settled.

Wafers spend weeks as work in process

A wafer does not move through one machine and emerge as a chip. It repeatedly travels through deposition, lithography, etch, cleaning, implantation, heat treatment and measurement. Intel describes a wafer’s fabrication journey as weekslong and involving thousands of processing steps.

Much of the elapsed time is not a machine actively changing the wafer. Lots wait for the next tool, measurement result, engineering review or rework decision. A tool can be busy, a chamber can be down or a process-control check can hold the lot before more value is added.

This creates work in process, or WIP: partially completed wafers moving through the factory. Starting more wafers today increases future output only after they clear the full route. If the fab is already constrained at a critical tool or inspection step, extra starts can create a longer queue rather than faster shipments.

The first wafer is evidence, not volume

When the first completed wafers return, the team tests individual dies. Some fail because of manufacturing defects. Others may reveal a design weakness that pre-silicon verification did not expose under real voltage, temperature or workload conditions.

Engineers call the first examination of real chips silicon bring-up. They check whether the device starts, communicates, runs software and meets power and performance targets. A serious error may require a new design revision, another tapeout and another trip through fabrication. A smaller issue may be managed with software, test screening or a manufacturing adjustment.

